A LETTER FROM THE FOUNDER
There are moments in technology when something changes.
Not because we invented another feature.
Not because we made an existing tool slightly faster.
But because we discover a new way to interact with what technology has already made possible.
I believe we are approaching one of those moments now.
And I believe the next great evolution of the internet won't simply be about having more information.
It will be about having more intelligence available to us—and being able to operate it naturally.
For decades, we've been building toward this.
And if you look closely, you can see the progression.

THE INTERNET WAS THE CAKE
The first great transformation was the internet.
We connected computers.
Then we connected networks.
Then we connected the world.
Suddenly information could travel across the planet almost instantly.
The internet became the foundation.
The cake.
It was extraordinary.
But most people still needed tools to navigate it.
So we built them.

GOOGLE WAS THE ICING
Then came search.
Google changed the relationship between humans and information.
Instead of knowing where information lived, we could simply ask:
"Where is it?"
Type something.
Search.
Find.
Click.
The world's information became dramatically easier to access.
That was enormous.
But we were still interacting with information primarily through interfaces.
We were searching the internet.
We weren't really having a relationship with it.

SOCIAL MEDIA WAS THE ICE CREAM
Then something different happened.
We didn't just connect to information.
We connected to each other.
Facebook.
YouTube.
Twitter.
Instagram.
TikTok.
Millions—and eventually billions—of people began sharing their lives, ideas, creativity, experiences and perspectives.
The internet became social.
It became a place where people didn't simply consume information.
They participated.
They created.
They connected.

MOBILE PUT THE INTERNET IN YOUR HAND
Then came the smartphone.
And this changed everything again.
The internet wasn't somewhere you went anymore.
You carried it.
In your pocket.
In your hand.
In your car.
At your desk.
At dinner.
On the street.
The world's information and communication infrastructure became available almost everywhere you went.
The internet became personal and portable.

THEN CAME AI
And then something extraordinary happened.
For the first time, we could begin to talk to the internet.
Instead of searching for information...
We could ask.
Instead of navigating menus...
We could describe what we wanted.
Instead of learning complicated software...
We could increasingly tell a machine what we wanted to create.
AI changed the interface.
The computer could finally begin to meet the human where the human was.
But something was still missing.

INTELLIGENCE WITHOUT CONTINUITY
Think about the experience most people have today.
You open ChatGPT.
You ask a question.
You get an incredible answer.
You start another conversation.
You work on something else.
You open another AI.
Another conversation.
Another context.
Another beginning.
And tomorrow?
You're often back at the starting line.
The intelligence is extraordinary.
But the continuity is fragmented.
And here's the strange part:
Humans have the same problem.
We forget.
We lose ideas.
We forget what we learned.
We forget why we made decisions.
We forget breakthroughs.
We forget lessons.
We forget what worked.
We forget what didn't.
We accumulate experiences...
but we don't necessarily accumulate the intelligence contained inside those experiences.

WHAT IF WE FIXED THAT?
That question is where Naya Power begins.
Not:
"How do we build a smarter AI?"
But:
"How do we build a better way for humans to operate extraordinary AI?"
That's a completely different problem.
Because I don't believe the world needs another AI model simply competing to be the smartest model.
The world needs a way for ordinary humans to use extraordinary intelligence extraordinarily well.

